-- Q1: medium / L3 — Two-class method effect on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redfield Industries reports net income of $1,200,000 with 400,000 weighted-average common shares and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock entitled to share equally in dividends with common shareholders. The company declared $300,000 in total dividends. Under the two-class method, what is the most likely effect on basic EPS compared to computing EPS without considering the participation feature?', '["Basic EPS is lower because undistributed earnings must be allocated between common and participating preferred shares", "Basic EPS is higher because the preferred dividends reduce the denominator rather than the numerator", "Basic EPS is unchanged because participating rights only affect diluted EPS", "Basic EPS is lower because all declared dividends are deducted from net income twice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method requires that undistributed earnings be allocated between common and participating securities based on their respective participation rights. This allocation reduces the numerator available to common shareholders, producing lower basic EPS than a simple deduction approach. Choice C is incorrect because participating securities affect basic EPS directly, not just diluted EPS, which is a frequent misconception under ASC 260.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Evaluate dilutive security inclusion order and compute diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greystone Holdings has net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares (basic EPS of $4.00). Three potentially dilutive securities exist: (1) stock options producing 5,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method, (2) $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock adding back $40,000 in dividends and 20,000 conversion shares, and (3) convertible bonds adding $30,000 after-tax interest and 10,000 shares. Evaluate the proper inclusion order and determine diluted EPS.', '["$3.90", "$3.73", "$3.70", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities must be ranked by per-share impact (most dilutive first): options $0/share, preferred $2.00/share ($40,000/20,000), bonds $3.00/share ($30,000/10,000). Step 1 (options): $800,000/205,000 = $3.90. Step 2 (preferred): $840,000/225,000 = $3.73. Step 3 (bonds): $870,000/235,000 = $3.70. Each step is dilutive, so all are included. Choice A ($3.90) is incorrect because it only includes options and stops the sequence prematurely.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Vanguard Solutions wrote put options obligating it to repurchase 20,000 of its own shares at $50 per share. The average market price during the year was $40 per share. Net income is $900,000 with 300,000 weighted-average shares outstanding. Evaluate whether these written puts are dilutive and recommend the appropriate treatment for diluted EPS under ASC 260.', '["The puts are dilutive; add 5,000 incremental shares to the denominator using the reverse treasury stock method", "The puts are antidilutive because the exercise price exceeds the market price and should be excluded", "The puts are dilutive; add 20,000 shares to the denominator at full count", "The puts are antidilutive; written puts never affect diluted EPS regardless of pricing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity issues shares at market to fund the repurchase: (20,000 x $50) / $40 = 25,000 shares issued, minus 20,000 repurchased = 5,000 net incremental shares added to the denominator. Choice B is incorrect because it applies the call-option dilution test in reverse—for puts, dilution occurs when exercise price exceeds market price.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — Convertible bonds issued mid-year partial-year if-converted effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, 2025, Belmont Corp. issues $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds at par. Each $1,000 bond converts into 30 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Net income for 2025 is $2,500,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares. What is the effect of these bonds on the diluted EPS numerator and denominator for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["Add $45,000 to the numerator and 30,000 shares to the denominator", "Add $22,500 to the numerator and 15,000 shares to the denominator", "Add $22,500 to the numerator and 30,000 shares to the denominator", "Add $45,000 to the numerator and 15,000 shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for mid-year issuances requires time-weighting. Interest savings = $1,000,000 x 6% x 6/12 = $30,000, net of tax = $30,000 x 0.75 = $22,500 added to the numerator. Conversion shares = 1,000 bonds x 30 shares x 6/12 = 15,000 added to the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because it uses full-year amounts without time-weighting for the July 1 issuance date.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares with mid-year treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mercer Dynamics has 240,000 shares of common stock outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, the company repurchases 40,000 shares as treasury stock. Net income for the year is $660,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["$2.75", "$3.30", "$2.93", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average must reflect the time period shares were outstanding. WACSO = (240,000 x 6/12) + (200,000 x 6/12) = 120,000 + 100,000 = 220,000. Basic EPS = $660,000 / 220,000 = $3.00. Choice A ($2.75) is incorrect because it divides by the beginning shares of 240,000, ignoring the time-weighting effect of the repurchase.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Shares issued in business combination weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, 2025, Oakmont Corp. acquires Sumner LLC by issuing 50,000 shares of common stock. Oakmont had 150,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. Net income for the year is $700,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["$3.50", "$4.67", "$4.00", "$3.78"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date, not retroactively. WACSO = (150,000 x 6/12) + (200,000 x 6/12) = 75,000 + 100,000 = 175,000. Basic EPS = $700,000 / 175,000 = $4.00. Choice A ($3.50) is incorrect because it treats the acquisition shares as outstanding for the full year, which applies only to stock dividends and splits, not business combinations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Stock warrants diluted EPS using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northbridge Corp. has 180,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and net income of $450,000 with no preferred stock. The company has 15,000 outstanding warrants with an exercise price of $24 per share. The average market price during 2025 was $40 per share. What is diluted EPS?', '["$2.50", "$2.42", "$2.31", "$2.37"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method computes incremental shares: proceeds = 15,000 x $24 = $360,000; shares repurchased = $360,000 / $40 = 9,000; incremental shares = 15,000 - 9,000 = 6,000. Diluted EPS = $450,000 / (180,000 + 6,000) = $450,000 / 186,000 = $2.42. Choice C ($2.31) is incorrect because it adds all 15,000 warrant shares without subtracting the assumed treasury stock repurchase.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Definition of simple capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what defines a simple capital structure for EPS reporting purposes?', '["A structure with fewer than 1,000,000 shares authorized", "A structure where all securities are publicly traded on a major exchange", "A structure with only one class of preferred stock outstanding", "A structure with only common stock and no potentially dilutive securities outstanding"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has only common stock outstanding and no potentially dilutive securities (such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments). Entities with simple structures report only basic EPS. Choice C is incorrect because having preferred stock—even one class—may create a complex structure if the preferred is convertible into common shares.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Contingently issuable shares with EPS-based condition and circular reference
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Inc. reports net income of $2,000,000 with 400,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. Under an acquisition agreement, 50,000 additional shares will be issued if EPS exceeds $4.50 in any of the next three years. Current-year basic EPS before considering the contingent shares is $5.00. The CFO must evaluate whether to include these shares in diluted EPS, noting that their inclusion would reduce EPS to $4.44. What is the correct treatment?', '["Include the 50,000 shares in diluted EPS; the resulting diluted EPS is $4.44", "Exclude the shares because the resulting diluted EPS of $4.44 would fall below the $4.50 threshold", "Include the shares only as an adjustment to the numerator, not the denominator", "Include the shares in basic EPS since the earnings condition is currently satisfied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if the conditions would be satisfied assuming the end of the reporting period is the end of the contingency period. Since current basic EPS of $5.00 exceeds the $4.50 target, the shares are included regardless of the resulting circular reduction. Diluted EPS = $2,000,000 / 450,000 = $4.44. Choice B is incorrect because the dilution test uses pre-inclusion EPS, not post-inclusion EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Retrospective restatement for stock split declared after period end
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. has 100,000 weighted-average common shares for the year ended December 31, 2025, and net income of $500,000. On January 15, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, the company declares a 2-for-1 stock split. What amount should be reported as basic EPS for 2025?', '["$5.00 because the split occurred after year-end", "$2.50 because the split is retroactively applied to the weighted-average shares", "$3.75 because the split is weighted for half the period", "$5.00 with pro forma disclosure of the post-period split"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in the EPS calculation. The WACSO is restated to 200,000 shares (100,000 x 2), and basic EPS = $500,000 / 200,000 = $2.50. Choice A is incorrect because post-period splits are not ignored—they require retroactive restatement of all periods presented.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — EPS presentation requirements for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Blackwell Industries reports income from continuing operations of $900,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $150,000 (net of tax), resulting in net income of $750,000. There are 300,000 weighted-average common shares with no preferred stock. Which EPS amounts must be presented on the face of the income statement?', '["$2.50 for net income only, with no separate continuing operations amount", "$3.00 for continuing operations, $(0.50) for discontinued operations, and $2.50 for net income on the face", "$3.00 for continuing operations and $2.50 for net income on the face of the income statement", "$3.00 for continuing operations only, with other amounts disclosed in the notes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present EPS for income from continuing operations ($900,000 / 300,000 = $3.00) and net income ($750,000 / 300,000 = $2.50) on the face of the income statement. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented either on the face or in the notes. Choice B is incorrect because discontinued operations EPS is not required on the face—it may be disclosed in the notes instead.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Share repurchase effect on basic EPS analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Drayton Holdings reported basic EPS of $4.00 last year based on net income of $1,200,000 and 300,000 weighted-average shares. In the current year, net income remains $1,200,000 but the company repurchased shares throughout the year, resulting in a weighted-average of 250,000 shares. What is the most accurate interpretation of the change in basic EPS?', '["EPS remains $4.00 because treasury stock repurchases do not affect the weighted-average share calculation", "EPS increases to $4.80, indicating improved company profitability year over year", "EPS decreases because repurchasing shares reduces total stockholders'' equity and earnings capacity", "EPS increases to $4.80, but the increase results from fewer shares rather than improved profitability, which analysts should evaluate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock repurchases reduce the weighted-average denominator. Basic EPS = $1,200,000 / 250,000 = $4.80, a 20% increase despite unchanged net income. Analysts should recognize that EPS growth from buybacks does not indicate improved operational performance. Choice B is incorrect because it attributes the EPS increase to improved profitability when net income was actually unchanged.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Denominator in basic EPS calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the denominator used to calculate basic earnings per share?', '["Total shares authorized in the corporate charter", "Shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "Total common and preferred shares outstanding combined", "Weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the denominator for basic EPS is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period. This calculation accounts for shares issued and repurchased at different dates throughout the year. Choice B is incorrect because using end-of-period shares would ignore the timing of share issuances and repurchases during the year.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Definition of dilutive security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best describes a dilutive security?', '["A security that pays dividends to preferred shareholders", "A security that has been outstanding for the entire reporting period", "A security whose assumed conversion or exercise would decrease earnings per share", "A security whose assumed conversion or exercise would increase earnings per share"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a dilutive security is one whose inclusion in the EPS calculation would decrease EPS (or increase loss per share). Only dilutive securities are included in diluted EPS; antidilutive securities are excluded. However, Choice D describes the opposite concept—an antidilutive security whose inclusion would increase EPS and must be excluded.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method applies to options and warrants
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, the treasury stock method is used to calculate the dilutive effect of which type of security?', '["Convertible bonds payable", "Stock options and warrants", "Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock", "Convertible preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method applies to stock options, warrants, and their equivalents. It assumes the proceeds from exercise are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price. Choice A is incorrect because convertible bonds use the if-converted method, which adds back after-tax interest to the numerator and conversion shares to the denominator.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Analyst error in if-converted method tax adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeview Corp. has net income of $500,000, 100,000 weighted-average common shares, and $200,000 of 8% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%). Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 common shares. An analyst computes diluted EPS by adding back $16,000 of interest and 5,000 conversion shares, yielding $4.91. What error, if any, did the analyst make?', '["The analyst correctly applied the if-converted method and $4.91 is accurate", "The interest add-back should be net of tax at $12,000, yielding diluted EPS of $4.88", "The full $16,000 interest is understated and the entire bond face value should be added back", "The bonds are antidilutive and should be excluded entirely from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method requires the after-tax interest add-back: $200,000 x 8% = $16,000 pretax, x (1 - 0.25) = $12,000 net of tax. Correct diluted EPS = ($500,000 + $12,000) / (100,000 + 5,000) = $512,000 / 105,000 = $4.88. The analyst added $16,000 pretax instead of $12,000 after-tax, overstating diluted EPS. Choice D is incorrect because $4.88 < $5.00 basic EPS, confirming the bonds are dilutive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred shares classified as liabilities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverton Corp. issued 25,000 mandatorily redeemable preferred shares classified as liabilities under ASC 480-10-25. The shares pay $200,000 in annual dividends recorded as interest expense. Net income (after this interest expense) is $1,500,000, and there are 300,000 common shares outstanding. What is the effect of the liability classification on the basic EPS calculation?', '["The $200,000 must still be deducted as preferred dividends, resulting in basic EPS of $4.33", "The $200,000 should be added back to net income because it was misclassified, resulting in basic EPS of $5.67", "The mandatorily redeemable shares must be included in the weighted-average denominator, reducing basic EPS", "No further numerator deduction is needed because the payments are already reflected as interest expense in net income, resulting in basic EPS of $5.00"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 480-10-25 and ASC 260-10-45-11A, mandatorily redeemable financial instruments classified as liabilities have their dividends treated as interest expense, already deducted in arriving at net income. No additional deduction is made in the EPS numerator. Basic EPS = $1,500,000 / 300,000 = $5.00. Choice A is incorrect because it double-counts the dividends by deducting them again as preferred dividends after they were already expensed as interest.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities regardless of size or ownership structure", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market", "All entities with more than 100 shareholders of record", "Only entities that have complex capital structures with dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public entities—those with common stock or potential common stock (such as options or convertibles) traded in a public market, or that have filed with a regulatory agency for the purpose of issuing securities. Choice A is incorrect because nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS, though they may do so voluntarily.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Net loss makes all potentially dilutive securities antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a company reports a net loss, how are potentially dilutive securities treated in the diluted EPS calculation?', '["They are included to show the maximum potential dilution to shareholders", "They are included only if they are convertible preferred stock", "They reduce the net loss by adding back interest and dividends in the numerator", "They are excluded because their inclusion would be antidilutive by reducing the loss per share"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, when a net loss exists, all potentially dilutive securities are considered antidilutive because adding shares to the denominator would reduce the loss per share, making results appear better than they are. Therefore, diluted EPS equals basic EPS in a net loss period. Choice A is incorrect because including additional shares during a loss period would decrease the reported loss per share, which is antidilutive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Stock dividend effect on comparative prior-year EPS restatement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bridgeway Corp. reported basic EPS of $6.00 for 2024 based on net income of $600,000 and 100,000 weighted-average shares. In September 2025, Bridgeway declares a 20% stock dividend. Net income for 2025 is $750,000 with a pre-dividend weighted-average of 100,000 shares. What are the comparative EPS figures?', '["2025 EPS is $6.25 and 2024 EPS is restated to $5.00", "2025 EPS is $6.25 and 2024 EPS remains at $6.00 as originally reported", "2025 EPS is $7.50 and 2024 EPS is restated to $5.00", "2025 EPS is $6.25 and 2024 EPS is restated to $4.80"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends require retroactive restatement of all prior-period EPS. The 2025 WACSO = 100,000 x 1.20 = 120,000; EPS = $750,000 / 120,000 = $6.25. The 2024 WACSO is also restated: 100,000 x 1.20 = 120,000; EPS = $600,000 / 120,000 = $5.00. Choice B is incorrect because prior-period EPS must be retroactively adjusted for stock dividends to maintain comparability.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether convertible preferred is antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. has net income of $180,000, 80,000 weighted-average common shares, and 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock. Each preferred share converts into 2 common shares. Preferred dividends of $40,000 were declared. The CFO computed basic EPS of $1.75. Evaluate whether the convertible preferred stock should be included in diluted EPS and determine the correct diluted EPS.', '["Exclude the preferred stock because it is antidilutive; diluted EPS equals basic EPS of $1.75", "Include the preferred stock; diluted EPS is $1.80", "Include the preferred stock; diluted EPS is $1.50", "Exclude the preferred stock; diluted EPS is $2.25"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, a convertible security is antidilutive if its inclusion would increase EPS. If-converted test: numerator = $180,000 (add back $40,000 dividends); denominator = 80,000 + 20,000 = 100,000. Result = $180,000 / 100,000 = $1.80, which exceeds basic EPS of $1.75. Since inclusion increases EPS, the preferred stock is antidilutive and must be excluded. Choice B is incorrect because it includes an antidilutive security, violating ASC 260-10-45-17.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Rising market price increases dilutive impact of stock options
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartwell Inc. has 500,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $2,000,000 with 50,000 stock options at an exercise price of $38. During Year 1, the average market price was $40, and during Year 2, it rose to $60 while net income and shares remained constant. What is the most likely effect of the market price increase on the dilutive impact of the options?', '["The dilutive impact increases because fewer shares are assumed repurchased at the higher market price under the treasury stock method", "The dilutive impact decreases because the higher market price makes the options less dilutive", "The dilutive impact is unchanged because the exercise price and number of options did not change", "The options become antidilutive in Year 2 because the market price significantly exceeds the exercise price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes proceeds from exercise are used to repurchase shares at the average market price. Year 1 incremental shares: 50,000 - (50,000 x $38/$40) = 2,500. Year 2 incremental shares: 50,000 - (50,000 x $38/$60) = 18,333. The higher market price means fewer shares repurchased with the same proceeds, increasing incremental shares and dilution. However, Choice B reverses the relationship—a wider spread between market and exercise price increases dilution, not decreases it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Cumulative preferred dividends in arrears for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Winslow Enterprises has net income of $900,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, and 10,000 shares of $100 par, 7% cumulative preferred stock. Preferred dividends have not been declared for the current year or the prior year. What is basic EPS for the current year?', '["$4.50", "$3.80", "$4.15", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, for cumulative preferred stock, the current period''s preferred dividend ($10,000 x $100 x 7% = $70,000) is deducted from net income regardless of whether declared. Prior-year arrearages are not deducted again. Basic EPS = ($900,000 - $70,000) / 200,000 = $4.15. Choice B ($3.80) is incorrect because it deducts two years of dividends ($140,000), including the prior-year arrearage that was already reflected in the prior year''s EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — If-converted method applies to convertible securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, the if-converted method for calculating diluted EPS applies to which type of security?', '["Stock options and warrants", "Treasury stock held by the issuing entity", "Convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock", "Contingently issuable shares under earnout agreements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method applies to convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock. It assumes conversion at the beginning of the period (or issuance date if later), adding back related interest or dividends to the numerator and conversion shares to the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because stock options and warrants use the treasury stock method, not the if-converted method.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L2 — Basic EPS with noncumulative preferred dividends declared
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearfield Corp. has net income of $480,000, 150,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding, and 5,000 shares of noncumulative preferred stock. Preferred dividends of $30,000 were declared and paid during the year. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.20", "$3.00", "$2.80", "$3.33"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, declared preferred dividends on noncumulative stock are deducted from net income in the EPS numerator. Basic EPS = ($480,000 - $30,000) / 150,000 = $450,000 / 150,000 = $3.00. Choice A ($3.20) is incorrect because it omits the preferred dividend deduction, dividing $480,000 by 150,000 and overstating EPS available to common shareholders.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Anti-dilution sequencing with antidilutive preferred excluded
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halford Enterprises has basic EPS of $5.00 based on net income of $750,000 and 150,000 weighted-average shares. Two potentially dilutive securities exist: (1) stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method, and (2) convertible preferred stock that would add back $60,000 in dividends and 10,000 conversion shares. Determine diluted EPS after proper sequencing.', '["$4.82", "$5.00", "$4.46", "$4.75"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested in order of most to least dilutive. Options ($0/share impact) are tested first: $750,000 / 158,000 = $4.75. Preferred ($60,000/10,000 = $6.00/share) tested next: $810,000 / 168,000 = $4.82. Since $4.82 > $4.75, including preferred would increase EPS, making it antidilutive at this step. Preferred is excluded. Diluted EPS = $4.75. Choice A ($4.82) is incorrect because it includes the antidilutive preferred stock, which should be excluded after failing the sequential dilution test.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Two-class method basic EPS calculation with participating preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. reports net income of $1,000,000. It has 200,000 common shares and 50,000 participating preferred shares that share equally in dividends with common shareholders. Total dividends declared are $250,000. Under the two-class method per ASC 260, what is basic EPS for common shareholders?', '["$5.00", "$3.75", "$4.00", "$3.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates both distributed and undistributed earnings. Distributed to common: $250,000 x (200,000/250,000) = $200,000. Undistributed earnings: $1,000,000 - $250,000 = $750,000; allocated to common: $750,000 x (200,000/250,000) = $600,000. Total common earnings = $800,000. Basic EPS = $800,000 / 200,000 = $4.00. Choice A ($5.00) is incorrect because it ignores the participation feature and uses full net income divided by common shares only.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares for shares issued for services mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Inc. has 180,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On August 1, it issues 12,000 shares to consultants as compensation for services rendered. Net income is $555,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["$3.08", "$2.89", "$3.00", "$2.78"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for services are included in the weighted-average from the issuance date. WACSO = (180,000 x 7/12) + (192,000 x 5/12) = 105,000 + 80,000 = 185,000. Basic EPS = $555,000 / 185,000 = $3.00. Choice A ($3.08) is incorrect because it uses only the beginning balance of 180,000 shares, ignoring the mid-year issuance in the weighting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — EPS presentation location on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, where must a public entity present basic and diluted EPS?', '["On the face of the income statement for all periods presented", "In the notes to the financial statements only", "In the statement of stockholders'' equity", "In the management discussion and analysis section of the annual report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, basic and diluted EPS must be presented with equal prominence on the face of the income statement for all periods for which an income statement is presented. This is a mandatory disclosure for public entities. Choice B is incorrect because EPS must appear on the face of the income statement, not relegated to the notes, although additional EPS disclosures may appear in the notes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Full dilution sequencing with three securities, one antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornberry Corp. has net income of $2,000,000, 500,000 weighted-average shares, and three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options producing 20,000 incremental shares, (2) $300,000 face, 8% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) converting into 12,000 shares, and (3) 10,000 shares of $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each converting into 1 common share. Evaluate which securities should be included in diluted EPS.', '["$3.72, including all three securities", "$3.64, excluding the convertible preferred because it is antidilutive in the sequential test", "$3.69, including only the stock options", "$3.84, excluding all securities as antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, rank by per-share impact: options $0/share, bonds $1.50/share ($18,000/12,000), preferred $8.00/share ($80,000/10,000). Basic EPS = ($2,000,000 - $80,000)/500,000 = $3.84. After options: $1,920,000/520,000 = $3.69. After bonds: $1,938,000/532,000 = $3.64. Testing preferred: $2,018,000/542,000 = $3.72. Since $3.72 > $3.64, preferred is antidilutive and excluded. Diluted EPS = $3.64. Choice A is incorrect because it includes the antidilutive preferred stock, which failed the sequential dilution test.', 'hard', 4)

;

COMMIT;